nodecode-undo is a rewrite of Moodtuner997's Moodtuner997/hermes-undo-effects:

  https://github.com/Moodtuner997/hermes-undo-effects
  commit f02de8ca69104f44b9b3ec78155d7152412b9e97

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Moodtuner997 (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the Hermes plugin's one `tool_execution` middleware, its
`/undo-effects` slash command and its `hermes undo-effects` CLI subcommand
became a Nodecode cell -- hooks around `write-file`, `edit` and `sh` (the three
verbs that change files in a mono-tool organism), and one `/undo` command. The
store keeps the original's shape: an entry per call that changed something, the
bytes content-addressed under the home, a restore that refuses a path edited
since and forces on request, and a snapshot taken before a restore so a restore
can itself be undone. The protection of credential-looking names (`*.pem`,
`id_*`, `.ssh`, ...) is kept as a list of names.

What was left out: Hermes's own tool arguments (its `write_file`, `patch`,
`terminal` and `execute_code` shapes and the shell parser they needed) are
replaced by Nodecode's three verbs and by reading one simple `rm` or `mv`;
`execute_code`'s "may have changed files" note has no counterpart because
Nodecode's eval is the only tool and its sh is hooked. Entries carry no session
id and no per-file mode, directories are not snapshotted, and there is no CLI
subcommand. The store writes s-expression manifests rather than JSON, since
nothing outside the cell reads them.
